{"kind":"assert","text":"Preserve documentation dependencies when publishing a merge\n\nRecommendation: make preservation of reviewed behavior dependencies a condition of merge publication. Authors should name the behavior they describe once; publishing the reviewed document should retain a usable connection to the corresponding published behavior. Start with one regression spanning two releases, before designing extra workflow.\n\nInventory provides a concrete failure. Its README candidate #320 cited behavior candidates. Merge published README #327, whose only direct basis was the merge receipt. The later v0.2.0 migration retired 32 artifacts, but none lay in #327’s provenance ancestry. README therefore remained fresh while still naming v0.1.2 and its old runtime digest. The correction is tracked by Inventory #503 and approved in #508. See the [frozen record evidence](publication-dependency-evidence.json) and [independent correction checks](inventory-readme-review-checks.json).\n\nThe current [publication builder](internal/mergeplan/mergeplan.go@33f69956167115d7cc3235b6b1864ed904f2802a:1293) gives each successor only the receipt as a direct basis. An [earlier design discussion](notes/2026-08-21-notes-and-decisions.md@33f69956167115d7cc3235b6b1864ed904f2802a:369) already identified loss of candidate-to-successor provenance in the related cross-path replacement case. This observation concerns missing edges: changing how staleness travels along existing edges cannot create the absent connection.\n\nThe acceptance example is small: publish a document and the behavior it explicitly describes together; later replace only that behavior. The unchanged document must then request re-checking, while an unrelated document stays fresh. A document already citing a live behavior publication must retain that dependency too. Keep exact-path matching, review authority and historical records intact; reject ambiguous dependency mapping instead of guessing from every artifact at the same head.\n\nThe next design should assess whether the sealed publication plan can carry these already-reviewed relationships to their published counterparts. Avoid another lifecycle state, duplicate author reports, or a manual repair request after every merge. This is a recommendation for Hugh’s review, not an adopted change to staleness policy or authorization to implement. Coordinate its design boundary with the existing #19659 recovery so it does not become a second competing staleness project.\n","body":{"head":"33f69956167115d7cc3235b6b1864ed904f2802a"}}